Title: A CHRISTMAS CAROL
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

Cold, dark, biting weather. Fog pours through every chink and keyhole.

EBENEZER SCROOGE sits at his desk counting money. He is a squeezing, wrenching, grasping, scraping, clutching, covetous old sinner—hard and sharp as flint.

In an adjoining cell, BOB CRATCHIT shivers over a single dying coal, his hands wrapped in a long white comforter.

FRED bursts into the room, ruddy, handsome, and glowing from walking in the frost.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with 'Merry Christmas' on his lips, should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart!

FRED
Uncle! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
I'll keep my Christmas humour to the last. So A Merry Christmas, uncle!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred smiles warm-heartedly at Bob Cratchit as he exits. Bob returns the greeting cordially.

A portly SOLICITOR enters, carrying credentials and a ledger.

SOLICITOR
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe? Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years?

SCROOGE
He died seven years ago, this very night.

SOLICITOR
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute, who suffer greatly at the present time.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

SOLICITOR
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

SOLICITOR
They are. Still, I wish I could say they were not.

SCROOGE
The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then? I'm very glad to hear it.

SOLICITOR
A few of us are endeavouring to raise a fund to buy the Poor some meat and drink, and means of warmth. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

SOLICITOR
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. I help to support the establishments I have mentioned; those who are badly off must go there.

SOLICITOR
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. Good afternoon, gentleman!

The Solicitor bows sorrowfully and withdraws.

Scrooge turns to Bob Cratchit.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December! But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

EXT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Fog and frost hang thick over the black old yard.

Scrooge reaches his heavy wooden door and inserts his key into the lock.

He looks up at the heavy iron door knocker.

The knocker glows with a dismal, livid light. Its metal features shift into the spectral face of JACOB MARLEY, with ghostly spectacles turned up on its forehead and motionless, dead eyes.

Scrooge stares fixedly, his blood turning cold. He blinks.

The face becomes an iron knocker once again.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

Scrooge turns the key sturdily, enters, and slams the door.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge double-locks the heavy door behind him. He sits beside a low fire in the grate, taking his gruel in dressing-gown and slippers.

A disused brass bell on the wall begins to swing softly, then violently. Every bell in the house rings out in chorus.

They cease simultaneously.

From deep below in the wine-merchant's cellar comes a heavy clanking sound—iron chains dragged over casks.

The cellar door booms open. Heavy steps and rattling metal climb the stairs, approaching Scrooge's door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

MARLEY'S GHOST passes straight through the heavy wooden door into the room.

The apparition is transparent. Bound around its waist is a long, heavy chain forged of cash-boxes, padlocks, ledgers, and steel purses.

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You don't believe in me.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Why do you doubt your senses?

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects them. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

Marley's Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain with an appalling noise. It unbinds the wrapper from its head, and its lower jaw drops down upon its breast.

Scrooge falls upon his knees, hands clasped before his face.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen; and if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard. Is its pattern strange to you? Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself? It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it since!

Scrooge looks around himself on the floor, terrified.

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business! Hear me! My time is nearly gone.

SCROOGE
I will. But don't be hard upon me, Jacob!

MARLEY'S GHOST
I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
Is that the chance and hope you mentioned, Jacob? I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One.

Marley's Ghost rebinds its jaw, walks backward toward the window—which floats open—and drifts out into the black night.

Scrooge rushes to the window. Outside, phantoms fill the air, moaning in chains.

Scrooge closes the window, staggers to his bed, and falls instantly asleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The room is pitch dark. The church bell outside strikes ONE with a deep, hollow stroke.

A flash of light fills the chamber. The bed-curtains are drawn aside.

Scrooge sits up, face to face with the visitor.

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands before him—a figure like a child, yet like an old man, in a white tunic trimmed with summer flowers, holding a holly branch. A jet of clear light shines from its head.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past. Your past. Rise, and walk with me!

The Spirit clasps Scrooge firmly by the arm, moving toward the window.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, upon your heart, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT (PAST)

The bedroom vanishes. Scrooge and the Spirit stand in a warm, bright warehouse filled with holly and roaring fires.

OLD FEZZIWIG, wearing a Welsh wig and massive waistcoat, laughs from his high desk.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick! Christmas, Ebenezer! Clear away, my lads, and let's have lots of room here!

YOUNG SCROOGE and DICK WILKINS clear the floor in seconds.

A fiddler plays. Dancers whirl. Old Fezziwig and MRS. FEZZIWIG lead the dance with incredible agility, Fezziwig cutting capers in the air.

Present Scrooge watches in ecstatic joy, laughing and clapping.

SCROOGE
Why, it's old Fezziwig! Bless his heart!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money. Is that so much?

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

Scrooge catches the Spirit's gaze and stops.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What is the matter?

SCROOGE
Nothing particular. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. COUNTING-HOUSE PARLOUR - DAY (PAST)

The warehouse dissolves into a cold parlour.

MIDDLE-AGED SCROOGE sits beside BELLE, a graceful young woman in mourning clothes.

BELLE
It matters little to you, very little. Another idol has displaced me; and if it can cheer and comfort you in time to come, as I would have tried to do, I have no just cause to grieve.

MIDDLE-AGED SCROOGE
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one. You fear the world too much. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you.

MIDDLE-AGED SCROOGE
I am not changed towards you. Am I?

BELLE
When our contract was made, you were another man. If this had never been between us, tell me, would you seek me out and try to win me now? A dowerless girl?

Middle-aged Scrooge remains silent.

BELLE
I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were. May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

Belle leaves him.

Present Scrooge covers his face in tears.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are shadows of the things that have been. That they are what they are, do not blame me!

Scrooge seizes the Ghost's extinguisher-cap and presses it down upon its head. Bright light streams out below as Scrooge sinks into sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The church clock tolls ONE.

A ruddy glow streams from the adjoining sitting room. Scrooge opens the door.

The room is transformed into a grove of living green hung with mistletoe. A roaring fire blazes. Heaped on the floor is a vast throne of poultry, roasts, mince-pies, fruit, and punch.

Reclining on the throne is the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT—a jovial Giant in a furred green robe, wearing a holly wreath.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in, and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me!

Scrooge approaches submissively.

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

A humble four-room house. MRS. CRATCHIT and BELINDA CRATCHIT lay the cloth.

BOB CRATCHIT enters carrying TINY TIM upon his shoulder. The boy carries a little wooden crutch, his limbs supported by an iron frame.

Bob places Tiny Tim by the hearth.

The family gathers round a tiny goose and a dish of mashed potatoes.

Bob serves hot spiced liquid into two tumblers and a custard-cup.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

Tiny Tim sits close to Bob, holding his father's hand.

Present Scrooge steps closer, watching anxiously.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no! Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head in deep penitence.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon!

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, the children! Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year!

EXT. CITY STREET - NIGHT

Scrooge and the Ghost of Christmas Present stand in the heavy snow.

The Ghost's hair has turned white; his face is old.

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe is very brief. It ends to-night at midnight. Hark! The time is drawing near.

The chimes strike three quarters past eleven.

Scrooge notices two hideous, withered children crouching beneath the Ghost's robe.

SCROOGE
Is it a foot or a claw?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Look down here! They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy!

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes TWELVE.

The Ghost of Christmas Present vanishes.

EXT. GRAVEYARD - NIGHT

Through the mist approaches the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME—a tall phantom shrouded in a deep black garment, leaving only one outstretched hand visible.

Scrooge bends upon his knee.

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come? Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But as I know your purpose is to do me good, lead on, Spirit!

The Phantom points silently forward.

INT. OLD JOE'S RAG SHOP - NIGHT

A filthy shop filled with rusty keys and rags. OLD JOE smokes a pipe.

MRS. DILBER and a CHARWOMAN enter with heavy bundles.

CHARWOMAN
Open the bundle, old Joe, and let me know the value of it.

Old Joe pulls out a heavy roll of dark cloth.

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains! You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

CHARWOMAN
Yes I do! Why not? Look through that shirt—they'd have wasted it, putting it on him to be buried in!

Old Joe pays out money from a bag. They laugh greedily.

CHARWOMAN
He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

Scrooge shudders in horror.

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY (FUTURE)

The house is quiet and dark. Mrs. Cratchit and her daughters sew by candle-light.

Bob Cratchit enters, wearing his long comforter. He sits in a small chair in the corner and wept quietly.

BOB CRATCHIT
My little, little child! My little child!

Scrooge watches him, weeping softly.

SCROOGE
Spectre, tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead?

EXT. GRAVEYARD - NIGHT

The Phantom stands among weed-grown graves, pointing down at a neglected headstone.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

The Phantom remains motionless and silent.

Scrooge creeps forward and reads the inscription: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no! Hear me! I am not the man I was. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life!

Scrooge clutches the Phantom's robe.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

The Phantom's hood shrinks down into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - DAY

Scrooge is on his knees, holding his own bedpost.

Sunlight streams through the window. Bells ring out across the city.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven and the Christmas Time be praised for this!

He scrambles up, laughing and sobbing, dressing himself wildly. He throws open the window.

A BOY in Sunday clothes passes below.

SCROOGE
Hallo, my fine fellow! What's to-day?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! The Spirits have done it all in one night. Do you know the Poulterer's in the next street? Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there?

BOY
What, the one as big as me? It's hanging there now.

SCROOGE
Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here! Come back with the man in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The Boy runs off like a shot.

SCROOGE
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim!

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - DAY

Fred and his wife sit with guests around a festive table.

Scrooge pokes his head timidly around the door.

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred leaps up and shakes Scrooge's hands with immense joy. The guests surround Scrooge with hearty greetings.

INT. SCROOGE'S COUNTING-HOUSE - MORNING

The clock strikes NINE. No Bob. A quarter past. No Bob.

At eighteen minutes past nine, Bob Cratchit rushes in, breathless, scrambling onto his stool.

SCROOGE
(feigning growl)
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time. It's only once a year, sir.

SCROOGE
Step this way, sir, if you please.

Bob enters Scrooge's office, trembling.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend. I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore—

Scrooge leaps up and gives Bob a playful dig in the waistcoat.

SCROOGE
—and therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob stares in utter disbelief.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! We will discuss your affairs this afternoon over a bowl of smoking bishop! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - NIGHT

A roaring fire lights the warm room.

Scrooge sits by the hearth with the Cratchit family. Tiny Tim sits contentedly on Scrooge's knee.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man, as the good old city knew.

TINY TIM
God bless us, Every One!

FADE OUT.

THE END
